@startuml 07-interfaces
' =====================================================================
' Key interfaces and extension points
' Traces to: FR-23, FR-24, FR-37..FR-40, NFR-3, NFR-4, NFR-11
'
' Shaped by AD-2: we ship a library, not a runner. The instructor's
' test suite calls us, and her run_autograder invokes our AI phase.
' There is no ITestRunner: running tests is not our job.
'
' IReportingClient is down to ONE method. Test results go to
' results.json through her own framework, so we never record outcomes.
' Her only call to us opts a target OUT of AI feedback, because
' ai_needed defaults to TRUE.
'
' ResultsPackager splices rather than authors: it reads the
' results.json her framework wrote and appends the AI feedback.
' =====================================================================

title Key Interfaces and Extension Points

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
' What the instructor writes. Not ours.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
package "Written by the instructor" #FFF2CC {
  class "Instructor Test Suite" as ITS #FFF2CC {
    Ordinary JUnit or pytest. Calls
    the reporting library with the
    target names registered in the
    web app.
    --
    Under the AUTOGRADER policy, the
    marking logic lives here: any
    condition she can express in code
    can decide whether a target gets
    AI feedback.
  }
}

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
' The library the instructor's suite calls -- Student C
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
package "Reporting Library (Student C)" #FBE6D4 {

  interface IReportingClient <<abstract>> {
    +ai_not_needed(target) : None
    +add_test_context(target, note) : None
    --
    The entire surface the instructor
    touches. Two optional calls. Test
    results go to results.json through
    her own framework, so there is no
    record_outcome.
    --
    add_test_context carries a diagnostic
    her test already computed into the
    prompt: the failing input, expected
    versus actual, which branch ran.
    --
    Initialises the run on first use:
    upserts the submission and creates
    one submission_target per registered
    target with ai_needed = TRUE.
  }

  class JavaReporter {
    Shipped as a jar for JUnit suites.
  }

  class PythonReporter {
    Shipped as a package for pytest.
  }

  class FakeReporter {
    Records calls in memory. Lets the
    instructor guide's examples and
    our own tests run offline.
  }

  IReportingClient <|.. JavaReporter
  IReportingClient <|.. PythonReporter
  IReportingClient <|.. FakeReporter

  note as N_TGT #FFF8DC
    **Validate the target name on the first
    call.** These identifiers cross a process
    boundary as bare strings, typed once in the
    web app and again in the suite. A mismatch
    must fail the instructor's test run loudly,
    not record an outcome against nothing.
    See OQ-12.
  end note
}

ITS ..> IReportingClient

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
' AI phase entrypoint -- Student C
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
package "AI Phase entrypoint (Student C)" #FBE6D4 {

  class AiPhaseEntrypoint {
    -client : IGradingClient
    -grader : AIGradingComponent
    --
    +main() : int
    --
    A command the instructor's
    run_autograder invokes after her
    test suite exits.
    --
    Asks the service for ONE action and
    performs it:
      GRADE   generate and splice
      DEFER   splice a "deferred" note
      REEMIT  splice stored feedback
    --
    It does not know the grading mode,
    the authorisation rule or the token
    guard. All three are derived
    server-side, so changing any of them
    needs no re-upload of the
    instructor's autograder zip.
    --
    Raw test outcomes are not an input.
    ai_needed says the target wants
    feedback, and test_context carries
    any diagnostic the instructor chose
    to pass us.
  }

  class ResultsPackager {
    +parse(path) : PlatformResults
    +splice(results, feedback) : dict
    +append_deferred(results) : dict
    +append_error(results, message) : dict
    --
    The only code that knows the
    platform's results.json schema.
    --
    Does NOT author results.json, and no
    model output shapes it. The
    instructor's framework wrote the
    file; this parses it, inserts the
    feedback string and score as values,
    and writes it back. Deterministic
    and unit-testable with no LLM in the
    loop.
    --
    append_error is the failure path: the
    student keeps their test results and
    an honest note that feedback could
    not be generated.
  }

  note as N_PKG #FFF8DC
    Splicing rather than authoring leaves the
    instructor's existing autograder setup intact.
    She keeps whatever produces her results.json
    today; we only add to it.

    It also means results.json is the only record
    of the functional test run, since nothing
    persists those outcomes. That is OQ-16.

    Note this class is the ONLY reader of the
    file. Prompts are built from the rubric, the
    student's source and test_context, so nothing
    needs to match a results.json entry to a
    target.

    The model's contribution is a string and a
    number, inserted by this class. It never
    emits JSON and never sees the file's
    structure. Splicing must stay deterministic:
    this is the code path between an LLM and a
    student's grade report.
  end note

  AiPhaseEntrypoint --> ResultsPackager
}

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
' Platform automation -- Student C
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
package "Platform Integration (Student C)" #FBE6D4 {

  interface ILearningPlatform <<abstract>> {
    +authenticate() : None
    +find_submission(platform_assignment_id,\n platform_submission_id) : SubmissionLocator
    +trigger_rerun(locator) : RerunResult
  }

  class GradescopePlatform {
    -page : playwright.Page
    -selectors : SelectorMap
  }

  class FakePlatform {
    Records rerun calls. Lets the
    trigger be tested with no browser
    and no account.
  }

  class SelectorMap {
    +login_email : str
    +login_password : str
    +assignment_rows : str
    +manage_submissions_link : str
    +submission_rows : str
    +submission_id_attr : str
    +rerun_autograder_button : str
    +rerun_confirm_button : str
    --
    Every CSS selector in one file.
    When Gradescope reskins, this is
    the only thing that changes.
  }

  class NightlyTrigger {
    -platform : ILearningPlatform
    -client : IGradingClient
    --
    +run() : TriggerRunSummary
    --
    Asks the service which submissions
    still have tasks marked ai_needed,
    then clicks rerun on each. Never
    calls an LLM.
  }

  ILearningPlatform <|.. GradescopePlatform
  ILearningPlatform <|.. FakePlatform
  GradescopePlatform --> SelectorMap
  NightlyTrigger ..> ILearningPlatform
}

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
' AI grading -- Student A. Runs INSIDE the autograder container.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
package "AI Grading (Student A)" #D6E4F0 {

  class AIGradingComponent {
    -providers : ILLMProvider[]
    -builder : PromptBuilder
    -comparator : ResponseComparator
    -estimator : ConfidenceEstimator
    -client : IGradingClient
    --
    +grade(task_id) : FeedbackItem
    --
    Same code in both modes. Only the
    caller's timing differs.
  }

  interface ILLMProvider <<abstract>> {
    +name : str
    +model : str
    +generate_feedback(req : FeedbackRequest) : FeedbackResponse
    +estimated_cost(req) : float
  }

  class ClaudeProvider
  class OpenAIProvider
  class GeminiProvider
  class FakeProvider {
    Deterministic canned responses, so
    the test suite runs in CI with no
    API key and no bill.
  }

  class PromptBuilder {
    +build(rubric, guidance,\n test_context, source) : str
    +version : str
    --
    test_context arrives as an ordered
    list of notes and is UNTRUSTED.
    Delimit it, label it as test output,
    cap its length, and keep the rubric
    authoritative over anything it says.
    Assertion messages routinely embed
    the student's own output.
  }

  class ResponseComparator {
    +compare(responses) : Comparison
    +select(responses) : FeedbackResponse
  }

  class ConfidenceEstimator {
    +estimate(responses) : float
  }

  ILLMProvider <|.. ClaudeProvider
  ILLMProvider <|.. OpenAIProvider
  ILLMProvider <|.. GeminiProvider
  ILLMProvider <|.. FakeProvider

  AIGradingComponent o--> "1..*" ILLMProvider
  AIGradingComponent --> PromptBuilder
  AIGradingComponent --> ResponseComparator
  AIGradingComponent --> ConfidenceEstimator
}

AiPhaseEntrypoint --> AIGradingComponent

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
' Service boundary -- Student B
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
package "Service Boundary (Student B)" #DCEFD9 {

  interface IGradingClient <<abstract>> {
    +init_run(platform_submission_id, submitted_at) : InitResult
    +set_ai_not_needed(submission_id, target) : None
    +add_test_context(submission_id, target, note) : None
    +get_action(platform_submission_id) : ActionResult
    +post_feedback(submission_id, target, result) : None
    +mark_ai_failed(submission_id, target, error) : None
    +mark_ai_complete(submission_id) : bool
    +get_selected_feedback(submission_id) : FeedbackPackage
    +list_submissions_pending_ai(mode) : SubmissionLocator[]
  }

  class HttpGradingClient {
    -base_url : str
    -assignment_token : str
    --
    Per-assignment bearer token,
    scoped to one assignment's rows.
    Never a DB credential.
  }

  class FakeGradingClient {
    In-memory. Lets the AI phase be
    tested offline.
  }

  interface IGradingRepository <<abstract>> {
    +get_assignment_config(...) : AssignmentConfig
    +upsert_submission(ref) : str
    +create_targets_default_needed(submission_id) : SubmissionTargetRef[]
    +set_ai_not_needed(submission_id, target) : bool
    +add_test_context(task_id, note) : None
    +decide_action(submission_id) : ActionResult
    +authorise_for_grading(submission_id) : None
    +get_test_context(task_id) : TestContext[]
    +save_feedback(submission_id, target, result) : None
    +mark_target_failed(submission_id, target, error) : None
    +mark_ai_complete_if_all_done(submission_id) : bool
    +get_selected_feedback(...) : FeedbackPackage
    +list_submissions_pending_ai(mode) : SubmissionLocator[]
  }

  class SqlGradingRepository
  class InMemoryRepository

  IGradingClient <|.. HttpGradingClient
  IGradingClient <|.. FakeGradingClient
  IGradingRepository <|.. SqlGradingRepository
  IGradingRepository <|.. InMemoryRepository
}

IReportingClient ..> IGradingClient : over HTTPS
AIGradingComponent ..> IGradingClient
AiPhaseEntrypoint ..> IGradingClient
NightlyTrigger ..> IGradingClient

note as N_ACTION #FFF8DC
  **Why one action endpoint rather than several
  queries.**
  In NIGHTLY mode the same autograder script runs
  twice and the database looks identical both
  times, so the container cannot tell Phase 1
  from Phase 3 locally. The trigger cannot tell
  it either: all the trigger does is click a
  button.

  So the trigger sets submission.ai_authorized_at
  before clicking, and the service derives the
  action from grading_mode, that flag and the
  token guard together.

  Keeping the derivation server-side is the point.
  The container ships inside the instructor's
  autograder zip, which is the hardest artifact
  in the system to update, and every adopting
  institution has its own copy.
end note

note as N1 #FFF8DC
  **Why IGradingClient and IGradingRepository
  are separate interfaces.**
  The repository is in-process SQL on our cloud
  host. The client is HTTPS from someone else's
  container. Collapsing them would mean shipping
  database credentials inside the autograder
  zip.

  Note the asymmetry decided in AD-4: the
  container DOES hold the instructor's LLM API
  key, and does NOT hold a database credential.
  It reaches our data only through this client,
  with a per-assignment token scoped to one
  assignment's rows.

  The Web API implements the client surface on
  top of the repository. Keeping them distinct
  is what makes that boundary visible in code
  rather than a deployment convention nobody
  remembers.
end note

note as N2 #FFF8DC
  **Cost control is now almost nothing.**
  ai_needed starts TRUE and the instructor's
  suite turns individual targets off. There is no
  resolution step to get wrong and no policy enum
  to interpret.

  What remains worth testing is that both
  set_ai_not_needed and add_test_context reject
  an unregistered target name. That is the one
  place a typo in her suite silently changes what
  gets billed or loses a diagnostic, and where
  Student D's tests earn their keep here.
end note

note as N3 #FFF8DC
  **Both outcomes must be recorded.**
  save_feedback and mark_target_failed each set
  completed_at on the row. A failure that left
  completed_at NULL would be picked up by the
  nightly trigger every night forever, since its
  query is exactly "ai_needed and completed_at
  IS NULL".

  mark_ai_complete then sets the submission guard
  once every ai_needed row has completed_at set,
  and returns whether it did.
end note

note as N4 #FFF8DC
  **Every interface ships with a fake.**
  FakeReporter, FakePlatform, FakeProvider,
  FakeGradingClient and InMemoryRepository are
  not extras. They are what lets four people
  work in parallel in week two rather than week
  six, and what makes TR-2 and TR-8 achievable
  with no Gradescope account and no API bill.

  Adding an abstract method means adding it to
  the fake in the same commit.
end note

@enduml
